Purpose and intended legitimate use
-----------------------------------
Reparse Lens is an experimental, offline-by-design Node.js command-line
diagnostic that inspects filesystem metadata in a directory selected by its
operator. It helps developers, build maintainers and users of coding agents
notice observable symbolic links, Windows junctions and path-scope
anomalies that can be absent from Git's ordinary file inventory. It can also
report Git worktree pointer uncertainty, hardlink ambiguity and optional
Windows filename portability conflicts.

This is a defensive auditing, debugging and compatibility tool. Its intended
use is on systems and directories the operator owns or is authorized to
inspect. It does not deliberately read ordinary file contents, follow
recognized links into their targets, execute Git or arbitrary commands, scan
networks, exploit vulnerabilities or transmit findings to a remote service.

Why this declaration is present
-------------------------------
Filesystem-link discovery and scope analysis are security-relevant techniques.
Results could be misused to identify interesting filesystem structures or
to plan unauthorized access attempts. This package is therefore voluntarily
declared as dual-use under npm's policy to provide transparency to maintainers,
users and registry reviewers. The declaration is not a security certification
or authorization to use the tool against third-party systems.

Limits and safety
-----------------
Reparse Lens is metadata-only and advisory: every machine-readable scan
sets authorization=false. A detected link can produce BLOCKED (exit 2),
but BLOCKED is a review signal, not a conclusion of maliciousness.
Even a clean Windows scan remains INCOMPLETE (exit 3), because exhaustive
native NTFS reparse-tag inspection is not implemented. The scanner cannot
prevent time-of-check/time-of-use changes, enforce filesystem permissions,
guarantee containment of another process, or certify a workspace safe.
Cloud, network/UNC and unusual filesystem providers may be incompletely
classified. Filesystem metadata can itself be sensitive; do not publish
unredacted reports containing private paths. Node.js and the operating system
may access remote resources while resolving metadata on network or cloud
filesystems even though the CLI has no network client.

Reparse Lens must never be used as a sandbox, access-control gate, malware
detector, or a substitute for platform security controls. Run it only with
appropriate authorization, review reported paths manually and follow the
documentation in README.md, SECURITY.md and docs/THREAT_MODEL.md.

Reporting and review
--------------------
Please disclose potentially exploitable findings privately according to
SECURITY.md. Do not include real secrets, private file inventories or
unauthorized access examples in public reports.

